   "type": "faq",
   "h": "Questions validation and quality teams ask",
   "items": [
    {
     "q": "Our specification says 3.1B certificates. Do you supply those?",
     "a": "3.1B is the designation from the 1991 edition of EN 10204. The 2004 edition replaced it with 3.1: an inspection certificate with specific test results for the delivered product, validated by an inspection representative independent of manufacturing. We supply 3.1 and can note the equivalence in the material log so your reviewer does not raise it. A type 2.2 test report is not accepted for product-contact parts."
    },
    {
     "q": "Which welder qualifications and sample weld procedures do you keep for product-contact tubing?",
     "a": "Welding procedures are qualified to ASME Section IX with a welding procedure specification (WPS) and procedure qualification record (PQR). Orbital operators hold a welding operator performance qualification (WOPQ); [number of qualified orbital operators]. A sample weld is made at the start of each shift and on any change of operator, power supply, weld head or tube size, examined and kept with the weld log."
    },
    {
     "q": "How do you tie 3.1 certificates to the physical tube and fittings?",
     "a": "Heat numbers are checked against the certificate when material arrives and [how components are marked or tagged]. Each weld log line names the heat numbers on both sides of the weld, so any weld traces back to its certificates."
    },
    {
     "q": "Do you borescope every weld?",
     "a": "Our standard is [share of product-contact welds borescoped, %] of product-contact welds, stated in the fabrication and inspection plan before work starts. ASME BPE allows a random sample; we expect most pharmaceutical customers to specify more. A weld that cannot be reached, such as a final closure weld, is accepted on its coupon and the reason is recorded against its number."
    },
    {
     "q": "What happens to a rejected weld?",
     "a": "It is cut out, not ground or re-fused. The replacement gets a new weld number, its own log line and inspection, and the log keeps the link to the rejected weld so the history is visible. We report the rejection rate in the dossier [state whether the weld rejection rate is reported]."
    },
    {
     "q": "Can our inspector witness fabrication, and can we audit the shop?",
     "a": "Yes. Hold and witness points are agreed in the fabrication and inspection plan, typically coupons, borescope review, pressure test and passivation. Supplier audits of the shop and its quality system are welcome before order. [Shop location, city and province.] See quality management."
    },
    {
     "q": "Are field welds recorded the same way as shop welds?",
     "a": "Yes. The same qualified operators, procedures, coupons, log format and borescope records apply on site, and field welds are added to the weld maps and dossier before SAT."
    },
    {
     "q": "Do we need electropolish?",
     "a": "Not by rule. SF1 mechanically polished is common for PW. We recommend SF4 electropolish for hot WFI, pure steam and tank interiors, where heat makes rouge more likely and a chromium-rich surface helps. Your URS or corporate standard decides; we state the finish on every component in the plan."
    },
    {
     "q": "Is passivation done in-house?",
     "a": "[State whether shop passivation and electropolishing are done in-house or by a named subcontractor, and whether site passivation is done by Aquemi staff.] Either way the certificate names the standard, chemistry, temperature, contact time and verification test."
    },
    {
     "q": "Who registers the pressure vessels?",
     "a": "Vessels are designed to ASME Section VIII, Division 1 and registered under CSA B51 with the authority in the province of installation, which issues the CRN. [State whether Aquemi holds an ASME certificate of authorization or uses a certified vessel fabricator, and the provinces where it has registered designs.] Fittings and, in some provinces, piping systems also need registration; we confirm what applies to your site at design."
    },
    {
     "q": "Are the control panels certified?",
     "a": "[State the panel shop, whether panels carry a certification mark to CSA C22.2 No. 286 or UL 508A, and whether field evaluation is used otherwise.] An uncertified panel can be stopped at site by the electrical inspector, so we settle this before order."
    },
    {
     "q": "Can we see a real weld log before we order?",
     "a": "[Confirm a redacted weld log and dossier index from a recent project can be shared under a non-disclosure agreement.] Ask with your fabrication spec review."
